WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.357
You're in a café, on its free Wi-Fi, paying a bill on your bank's website.

2
00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:14.732
Every packet you send goes first through the little Wi-Fi box behind the counter, and it can read anything that passes.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.847
And yet, in less than a fifth of a second, a padlock appears.

4
00:00:19.133 --> 00:00:25.668
So how do your laptop and the bank agree on a secret key while the box reads every word, and that fast?

5
00:00:25.867 --> 00:00:30.627
It's tempting to think the padlock hides everything, from the very first word.

6
00:00:30.627 --> 00:00:31.725
Hold that thought.

7
00:00:31.967 --> 00:00:35.395
Let's put the bank 70 milliseconds away, there and back.

8
00:00:35.395 --> 00:00:39.191
That's one round trip, and every wait for an answer costs one.

9
00:00:39.433 --> 00:00:46.352
First, the two programs open a TCP connection, the protocol underneath that delivers bytes in order.

10
00:00:46.352 --> 00:00:48.428
That's one round trip already.

11
00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:51.040
Now, the careful way to set up a lock.

12
00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:54.674
Ask which kinds of key exchange the bank accepts, and wait.

13
00:00:54.674 --> 00:00:57.445
Then send your half, and wait for the bank's.

14
00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:01.279
Two more round trips before your payment can leave.

15
00:01:01.279 --> 00:01:04.647
TLS 1.2, the version before, needed two as well.

16
00:01:04.933 --> 00:01:07.116
Wouldn't it be nice to skip the asking?

17
00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:08.952
Picture a café abroad.

18
00:01:08.952 --> 00:01:12.392
You're not sure if the cashier speaks English or French.

19
00:01:12.667 --> 00:01:14.245
You could ask, and wait.

20
00:01:14.245 --> 00:01:18.191
Instead, you say your order twice, in English and in French.

21
00:01:18.191 --> 00:01:20.164
The cashier answers in French.

22
00:01:20.164 --> 00:01:21.611
One whole wait, saved.

23
00:01:21.833 --> 00:01:22.756
Let's map it.

24
00:01:22.756 --> 00:01:27.372
Each language is a kind of key exchange, which TLS calls a group.

25
00:01:27.633 --> 00:01:34.639
Your order in each language is a key share, your half of the exchange, made in advance for each group you guess.

26
00:01:34.639 --> 00:01:37.204
The cashier's answer is the bank's reply.

27
00:01:37.467 --> 00:01:42.218
So your laptop's first message, the ClientHello, carries the guesses.

28
00:01:42.218 --> 00:01:48.552
It names the server, api.pay.test, lists the methods it accepts, and carries two key shares.

29
00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:51.544
The bank answers with the ServerHello.

30
00:01:51.544 --> 00:01:54.504
It picks one group, and sends its own key share.

31
00:01:54.767 --> 00:01:58.849
Here the café picture stops: saying it twice makes nothing secret.

32
00:01:58.849 --> 00:02:01.942
The secret comes from this lesson's first section.

33
00:02:01.942 --> 00:02:05.901
Each side keeps a secret at home, and sends only a public value.

34
00:02:06.133 --> 00:02:11.709
Each mixes its own secret with the other's public value, and both get the same key.

35
00:02:11.709 --> 00:02:14.531
The box saw both public values, and can't.

36
00:02:14.733 --> 00:02:17.513
And notice, the box read both hellos.

37
00:02:17.513 --> 00:02:24.048
The padlock doesn't hide the first words: the box knows you're talking to api.pay.test.

38
00:02:24.267 --> 00:02:26.932
So we have a key the box can't work out.

39
00:02:26.932 --> 00:02:27.731
Are we done?

40
00:02:27.731 --> 00:02:30.196
Against a box that only listens, yes.

41
00:02:30.467 --> 00:02:32.077
But the box can also talk.

42
00:02:32.077 --> 00:02:36.104
It can play the bank to your laptop, and your laptop to the bank.

43
00:02:36.104 --> 00:02:38.892
Two keys, and it reads everything in between.

44
00:02:39.133 --> 00:02:40.719
Both locks work perfectly.

45
00:02:40.719 --> 00:02:43.098
They lock you in with the wrong person.

46
00:02:43.098 --> 00:02:46.940
A lock on its own proves nothing about who holds the other key.

47
00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:50.164
That's what the rest of the bank's reply is for.

48
00:02:50.164 --> 00:02:52.640
Everything after the ServerHello is locked.

49
00:02:52.900 --> 00:03:03.317
First, the certificate, the bank's passport from the last section: this public key belongs to api.pay.test, signed by a certificate authority.

50
00:03:03.567 --> 00:03:05.607
But anyone can copy a passport.

51
00:03:05.607 --> 00:03:12.715
So the bank also signs every byte of the conversation so far, with a private key that never leaves the bank.

52
00:03:12.715 --> 00:03:14.360
That's CertificateVerify.

53
00:03:14.567 --> 00:03:18.884
Last, Finished: a short code worked out from every byte so far.

54
00:03:18.884 --> 00:03:23.817
If the box had changed one word of the hellos, the codes wouldn't match.

55
00:03:24.067 --> 00:03:27.609
The box has a copy of the passport, but not the private key.

56
00:03:27.609 --> 00:03:32.037
It can't make the signature, so your laptop's check fails, and it hangs up.

57
00:03:33.267 --> 00:03:35.660
With the real bank, every check passes.

58
00:03:35.660 --> 00:03:41.920
Your laptop sends its own Finished, with your payment in the same flight, and new keys lock your data.

59
00:03:42.167 --> 00:03:46.000
So how long before the bank sees the first byte of your payment?

60
00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:46.959
Pause and guess.

61
00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:52.176
TCP, 70 milliseconds.

62
00:03:52.176 --> 00:03:55.941
The two hellos, one round trip, 70 more.

63
00:03:55.941 --> 00:03:59.329
Then the payment's one-way trip, 35.

64
00:03:59.329 --> 00:04:01.588
That's 175 milliseconds.

65
00:04:01.588 --> 00:04:04.505
The careful way would take 245.

66
00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:12.156
This opening exchange is the TLS handshake: the messages two programs swap before any real data.

67
00:04:12.156 --> 00:04:16.660
TLS 1.3 needs one round trip, because your laptop guesses.

68
00:04:16.900 --> 00:04:18.120
Three guardrails.

69
00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:26.519
A wrong guess costs one more round trip: the bank answers with a HelloRetryRequest, and your laptop says hello again.

70
00:04:26.519 --> 00:04:32.621
That's why clients always send a share for x25519, which nearly every server accepts.

71
00:04:32.867 --> 00:04:35.672
Every new connection pays both round trips.

72
00:04:35.672 --> 00:04:41.349
So programs keep a connection pool, a set of open connections they reuse, and pay once.

73
00:04:41.633 --> 00:04:43.270
And the hellos are open.

74
00:04:43.270 --> 00:04:47.769
The box still sees the name, the addresses, and how much you send.

75
00:04:48.033 --> 00:04:50.650
So: two open hellos carry the guesses.

76
00:04:50.650 --> 00:04:56.985
Everything after is locked, and a signature only the real bank can make proves who answered.

77
00:04:57.233 --> 00:04:59.277
Guess, so you don't have to ask.

78
00:04:59.277 --> 00:05:01.449
Sign, so the lock means something.

79
00:05:02.033 --> 00:05:03.042
One quick check.

80
00:05:03.042 --> 00:05:06.255
The bank accepts neither group your laptop guessed.

81
00:05:06.255 --> 00:05:08.838
How long before it sees your payment now?

82
00:05:08.838 --> 00:05:09.847
Pause and think.

83
00:05:13.067 --> 00:05:18.189
245 milliseconds: one extra round trip for the HelloRetryRequest.

84
00:05:18.189 --> 00:05:22.680
A wrong guess costs exactly what asking first would have.
